*Executive summary.* When a tube preamplifier, studio console or vintage hi-fi unit fails, a technician must find which of its many parts failed, a paid bench evaluation at the specialist shops we sampled. For a circuit it has a model of (@fig:concept), Differential simulates how every cataloged part failure changes the readings, keeps a probability for every group of suspects that readings cannot tell apart, and recommends the measurement expected to narrow them most per unit of effort. A language model reads the complaint and explains; readings come from the technician; safety locks run in code. In a pilot on simulated units it named the group containing the failed part first on << pct(bt.T1.estimate, 1) >> of as-new and << pct(bt.T6.estimate, 1) >> of aged units, with << num(bt.T9.estimate, 2) >> times half-split tracing's effort to a confirmed answer. The primary targets, fixed before any test unit is run (@tab:targets), include at least << num(T['T1'].pass_count, 0) >> of << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> simulated units right, << num(T['T6'].pass_count, 0) >> of << num(ev.aged_cases, 0) >> aged ones and << T['T7'].pass_count >> of << ev.fault_board_min >> faults on real boards, plus margins over scripts and a chat model; each will be reported met or missed.

