<< part(1, "Problem, stakeholders and industry context") >>

*The problem.* Studio consoles, tube preamplifiers and vintage hi-fi are repaired long after their makers stop supporting them, and finding the failed part is the slow step: U.S. defense maintainability guidance holds that most repair time typically goes to fault isolation << cite('waru_acquipedia_rtat') >>, a premise the ticket coding below will test for audio benches. Differential, proposed here, helps a qualified bench technician find the part: it simulates the unit's circuit to rank the parts that could have failed and to choose each next measurement. Specialist shops we sampled charge \$125 to \$150 for a bench evaluation << cite('rates_studio_doctors', 'rates_archer_hifi', 'rates_cw_guitar_audio') >>, and one bills an hour even when no fault is found << cite('rates_british_audio') >>; these are examples, not a survey.

*Size of the need.* No statistic counts audio repair: its U.S. industry code was merged into a broader one in 2022 << cite('census_naics_2017_to_2022_concordance') >>, and the closest occupation (24,720 people in May 2023) works mainly in retail and construction << cite('bls_oews_492097_2023', 'onet_492097_details') >>. The broader electronics repair group (117,700 jobs in 2025) is projected to grow 2% to 2035, below the 3.5% average << cite('bls_ooh_eeir', 'bls_ep_2025_35') >>. Only press reports speak for the specialist niche: skilled hi-fi technicians "are scarce, and their numbers are dwindling" << cite('stereophile_retales_13') >>, and a Chicago shop for old audio gear shrank from seven technicians to two << cite('blockclub_electronic_engineers_2026') >>. An AI assistant raised support agents' productivity 15%, most for the least experienced << cite('brynjolfsson_genai_at_work_2023') >>, a case for supporting newer technicians, not replacing them.

*Where it pays.* A model needs the schematic transcribed (we will time one) and, for our << cs.components >>-part channel strip, << m('library.setup_cost.channel_strip.total_minutes') | round | int >> minutes of computer time, so it pays where one model serves many units, such as a console of identical channel strips.

*Where current AI stands.* Maintenance AI works from records and documents (service histories << cite('ptc_servicemax_ai_2025') >>, manuals << cite('msft_d365_field_service_page') >>, repair guidance paired with failure prediction << cite('siemens_maintenance_copilot_2025') >>, agentic workflows << cite('ibm_mas_92_2026') >>, repair guides << cite('ifixit_fixbot_2025') >>); none of these five simulates the circuit. The best chat model scores 95.50% on textbook analog questions, graded by another language model << cite('razavi_bench_2026') >>, but at the bench parts sit anywhere within tolerance, faults look alike, measurements cost effort and some points carry lethal voltage. A pipeline that added a circuit-diagram detector and a simulator check to a language model lifted it from 79.52% to 97.59% on undergraduate circuit problems << cite('e2e_circuit_analysis_2025') >>; Differential brings such a check to diagnosis.

*Jurisdiction and rules.* We design for the U.S. (the team is in New Jersey) and check EU rules for later sale. No federal AI statute regulates such a tool, but the FTC has acted against unsupported AI accuracy claims << cite('ftc_workado_final_order_2025') >>. Texas's AI act (in force since 2026) binds it only through bans on intentional misuse, such as inciting harm or discriminating << cite('tx_hb149_traiga_codified') >>; Colorado's covers automated decisions about people, such as employment, from 2027 << cite('co_sb26_189') >>, one reason our terms of use forbid rating staff; and the Administration has asked Congress to preempt state AI laws it deems unduly burdensome << cite('wh_ai_legislative_framework_2026') >>. The EU AI Act would not deem a bench aid high-risk unless shops rated staff or trainees with it << cite('eu_ai_act_text_mirror') >>; users must be told they deal with an AI from 2 August 2026 << cite('ec_ip_26_1714') >>, and the 2026 amendments keep the duty to support staff's AI literacy << cite('eu_ai_omnibus_2026_1744') >>.

