*How it will be measured.* The test set holds << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> single-fault units (<< num(ev.channel_strip_cases, 0) >> channel strip, << ev.cases_per_block >> per block) and << num(ev.aged_cases, 0) >> aged units with the same mix, on seeds disjoint from training (aging within the training ranges; harsher aging meets the tool only in the tolerance curve and on the boards). Faults count by how often they cause a symptom, since a unit without one is redrawn. Complaints come from a phrase bank the reader was not developed on (its score was seen once, before a rule change; T18 uses a sealed bank), in three voices (a bench technician, a hobbyist owner, a studio client), never naming a part, each symptom dropped with probability << m('library.complaint_noise.p_omit_pct') | round | int >>% and others added with << m('library.complaint_noise.p_add_pct') | round | int >>%. Stress sets: << num(ev.unmodeled_cases, 0) >> units outside the catalog (double faults, wrong-value parts, solder bridges) and tolerances 1.5-3 times wider (a curve). Baselines: a fixed-order service chart, half-split tracing (halving the signal path with a tone; the strongest script) and random probing (never unsolders) share the engine's inference and stopping rule, and the scripts unsolder their leading suspect at << pct(m('library.design.confirm_at'), 0) >> belief; a language model, alone and with our simulator as a tool, picks from the same list on << ev.llm_subset >> stratified units and stops when it names an answer. A healthy-channel comparison, usual in stereo gear, joins them at M2 without a bar (it needs twin units on a shared supply). For T7 a teammate who does not record draws << ev.fault_board_min >> buildable faults at random and inserts them in sealed order into three 24 V line-driver boards, one built from used parts; each point is recorded once per fault and every method is replayed on the recordings. Study S1 will time at least six technicians with and without the too