*Scope.* In: single static faults (opens, shorts, drift, wear) and the hum and distortion they cause, in supplies, preamplifier and line-level stages. Out (non-goals): power amplifiers, intermittent and mechanical faults, double faults (flagged only), unpowered checks and visual inspection (not yet modeled), consumer self-repair, mains-side work, unattended probing. *Plan:* interviews and ticket coding by M2 (week 8); the boards, T7 and the full evaluation by M3 (week 11); S1 and the live runs by M5 (week 13). If the boards or the ethics review slip, T7 or S1 is reported as pending, never replaced.

#decision("Score by ambiguity group and effort to a confirmed answer")[
*Options:* per-part accuracy and a count of measurements, or group accuracy and weighted effort to a confirmed answer. *Choice:* the latter: per-part scoring rewards lucky guesses between faults with identical readings, unsoldering costs more than probing, and an answer is worth acting on only once confirmed. *Consequence:* group sizes and raw effort are also reported.
]

<< part(4, "Preliminary AI approach, risks and ethics") >>

*Why a hybrid.* Language models guess when uncertain, partly because training and evaluation reward guessing << cite('kalai_why_hallucinate_2025') >>. Alone, a model has no expected readings for this unit under tolerance, no calibrated belief over hundreds of suspects and no cost per measurement; with a simulator as a tool << cite('e2e_circuit_analysis_2025') >> it checks one guess at a time but lacks the last two (T5 and the model-with-simulator comparison test this). Simulation supplies the physics; Bayesian inference keeps a probability for every suspect group, updated after each reading through likelihoods learned from simulated units: one to three full-covariance Gaussians per fault (chosen by the Bayesian information criterion) over the circuit's readings, giving a likelihood for any subset of readings in closed form. "No single fault fits" is a hypothesis with a broad flat density, set on validation units to flag at most << pct(m('library.design.false_alarm_budget'), 0) >> of single faults. Boosted trees << cite('ke_2017_lightgbm') >> are a comparison engine (pilot top-1 << pct(m('bars.context.engine_disc_top1_weighted'), 1) >> against << pct(m('bars.context.accuracy_weighted.engine'), 1) >>). The next measurement maximizes expected information gain per unit of effort, the one-step entropy rule of model-based diagnosis << cite('dekleer_williams_1987') >> with a cost term (an optimal sequence needs a search << cite('pattipati_alexandridis_1990') >>); a diagnosis stops at << pct(m('library.design.stop_threshold'), 0) >> belief in one group, at the budget, or when nothing informative remains. The language model (Anthropic's Claude, version pinned) reads complaints (offline, rules do), proposes meter-photo readings for confirmation (offline, image processing does) and explains; every number it writes must trace to a reading (T17), and it stays only if T5, T18 and T19 show it helps.

*Set aside:* consistency-based candidates << cite('dekleer_williams_1987') >> (tolerances blur conflicts); retrieval over manuals (they describe healthy units); hand-built troubleshooting networks (one per product); deep networks that condition on any subset of readings << cite('ivanov_vaeac_2019', 'li_acflow_2020') >> and choose measurements by information gain << cite('ma_eddi_2019') >>, but are harder to audit than one density per fault.

*Autonomy.* Levels, by the functions automated << cite('parasuraman_sheridan_wickens_2000') >>: L0, none; L1, it ranks suspects; L2, it also recommends each step; L3, it takes readings with approval; L4, unattended. Differential is L2: a person takes and confirms every reading.

*Safety.* A hazard map lists every test point that can exceed 50 V, healthy or faulted, and a fault can put high voltage where there is normally none: channel-strip point << hv.showcase.tp >>, normally about << num(hv.showcase.normal_max_v, 0) >> V, reaches << num(hv.showcase.worst_case_v, 0) >> V under "<< hv.showcase.fault_label >>". Steps at points above 50 V carry hands-off and discharge warnings; in a high-voltage chassis every powered step says "high voltage inside", names a meter rated << design.safety.meter_rating >> and waits until the technician records how the unit was brought up (variac, series lamp, current-limited supply, or already running). Before a part is removed, the owner's approval is recorded and the technician records under << num(m('library.design.discharge_verify_max_v'), 0) >> V at every point that can hold high voltage; the check lapses after << design.safety.discharge_valid_min | round | int >> minutes without a bleeder, as a discharged capacitor recovers charge << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>, and on any power-up. These are checks, not guarantees: the locks act on typed readings, so they bound what the tool recommends and records, not what a person does; the residual shock risk rests on the user's qualification, and the hazard map covers only the modeled circuit.

*People.* Runner-up suspects and reasons are shown, yet AI can mislead: consultants given GPT-4 were 19 percentage points less likely to solve a task outside its competence << cite('dellacqua_jagged_frontier_2023') >>, and those trusting AI more report less critical thinking << cite('lee_critical_thinking_2025') >>. Trainees commit their own next step before seeing the tool's and need a named supervisor near high voltage; a tutor giving hints instead of answers largely mitigated the learning loss an unrestricted chat model caused << cite('bastani_genai_learning_2025') >>. S1 plants one wrong recommendation on a non-hazardous step of a 24 V board, after an ethics-board determination; participants are told beforehand that some advice may be wrong, are debriefed and recruited outside their employers. Whether owners turn faster diagnosis into a faster pace is a question for the interviews. As fairness audits do for groups of people << cite('buolamwini_gebru_2018') >>, errors will be broken down by complaint voice, since owners who describe faults in plain words could get worse diagnoses (non-English complaints are not covered); breakdowns by fault type and circuit show testability, not fairness. A gap above 5 points on a primary whose 95% interval excludes zero blocks release until explained.

*Accountability.* The team answers for the hazard map, stated performance and corrections; a shop, for its circuit models and staff; the technician, for measurements and precautions. Responsibility should follow control, lest the technician become a moral crumple zone, blamed for a system they cannot steer << cite('elish_2019_moral_crumple_zones') >>: the ticket records what the tool recommended, why, and what the technician attested. Whether advice software is a product (strict liability) or a service (negligence) is unsettled in the U.S.; bills would make AI systems products << cite('us_ai_lead_act_s2937', 'us_s2937_ai_lead_act_billstatus') >> or shield developers when licensed professionals use them << cite('us_rise_act_s2081') >>; the revised EU directive makes software placed on the market after 9 December 2026 a product << cite('eu_pld_2024_2853') >>. California bars anyone who developed or used an AI from answering a harm claim by saying the AI acted autonomously << cite('ca_ab316_2025_chaptered') >>.

